cription":"chemical compound","kind":null,"atc":"J02AX06","mesh":"D000077612","uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:39+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":"CCCCCOC1=CC=C(C=C1)C2=CC=C(C=C2)C3=CC=C(C=C3)C(=O)N[C@H]4C[C@H]([C@H](NC(=O)[C@@H]5[C@H]([C@H](CN5C(=O)[C@@H](NC(=O)[C@@H](NC(=O)[C@@H]6C[C@H](CN6C(=O)[C@@H](NC4=O)[C@@H](C)O)O)[C@@H]([C@H](C7=CC=C(C=C7)O)O)O)[C@@H](C)O)C)O)O)O","chembl_phase":4.0,"first_approval":2006,"bbw":0,"fda":1,"ema":1,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:48:37+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"For use in the treatment of the following fungal infections: Candidemia and other forms of Candida infections (intra-abdominal abscess, and peritonitis), Aspergillus infections, and esophageal candidiasis. Also considered an alternative treatment for oropharyngeal canaidiasis.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00362","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"FDA Label","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00362","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","items":[{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Substances that destroy fungi by suppressing their ability to grow or reproduce. They differ from FUNGICIDES, INDUSTRIAL because they defend against fungi present in human or animal tissues.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0001399.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Drug Classes","items":[{"section":"Drug Classes","text":"Breast Feeding; Lactation; Milk, Human; Antifungal Agents; Echinocandins","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/anidulafungin/","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"Anidulafungin is a semi-synthetic lipopeptide synthesized from a fermentation product of <i>Aspergillus nidulans</i>. Anidulafungin is an echinocandin, a class of antifungal drugs that inhibits the synthesis of 1,3-&beta;-D-glucan, an essential component of fungal cell walls. Anidulafungin is active in vitro against many <i>Candida</i>, as well as some <i>Aspergillus</i>. Like other echinocandins, anidulafungin is not active against <i>Cryptococcus neoformans</i>, <i>Trichosporon</i>, <i>Fusarium</i>, or zygomycetes.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00362","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"Anidulafungin is a semi-synthetic echinocandin with antifungal activity. Anidulafungin inhibits glucan synthase, an enzyme present in fungal, but not mammalian cells. This results in inhibition of the formation of 1,3-&beta;-D-glucan, an essential component of the fungal cell wall, ultimately leading to osmotic instability and cell death.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00362","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Less than 1% of the administered radioactive dose was excreted in the urine. Anidulafungin is not hepatically metabolized.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00362","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"30 to 50 L","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00362","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"1 L/h","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00362","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Protein Binding","items":[{"section":"Protein Binding","text":"84%","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00362","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Hepatic metabolism of anidulafungin has not been observed. Anidulafungin is not a clinically relevant substrate, inducer, or inhibitor of cytochrome P450 (CYP450) isoenzymes. Anidulafungin undergoes slow chemical degradation at physiologic temperature and pH to a ring-opened peptide that lacks antifungal activity.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00362","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Hepatic metabolism of anidulafungin has not been observed. Anidulafungin is not a clinically relevant substrate, inducer, or inhibitor of cytochrome P450 (CYP450) isoenzymes. Anidulafungin undergoes slow chemical degradation at physiologic temperature and pH to a ring-opened peptide that lacks antifungal activity.\nRoute of Elimination: Less than 1% of the administered radioactive dose was excreted in the urine. Anidulafungin is not hepatically metabolized.\nHalf Life: 40-50 hours","source":"Toxin and Toxin Target Database (T3DB)","url":"http://www.t3db.ca/toxins/T3D2763","license_url":"https://t3db.ca/downloads"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"40-50 hours","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00362","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]}],"safety":[{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","items":[{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","text":"◉ Summary of Use during Lactation Because of the low levels of anidulafungin in breastmilk, amounts ingested by the infant are small and would not be expected to cause any adverse effects in breastfed infants. If anidulafungin is required by the mother, it is not a reason to discontinue breastfeeding. ◉ Effects in Breastfed Infants A postpartum woman was treated with intravenous anidulafungin 100 mg once daily for 14 days. She withheld breastfeeding until the second day after completing her 14-day course of therapy. Her milk had detectable anidulafungin levels until 32 hours after the last dose. No gastrointestinal or other adverse effects were seen in the infant up to 72 hours after the last dose of drug. ◉ Effects on Lactation and Breastmilk Relevant published information was not found as of the revision date.","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/anidulafungin/"}]},{"section":"Toxicity Summary","items":[{"section":"Toxicity Summary","text":"Anidulafungin is a semi-synthetic echinocandin with antifungal activity. Anidulafungin inhibits glucan synthase, an enzyme present in fungal, but not mammalian cells. This results in inhibition of the formation of 1,3-&beta;-D-glucan, an essential component of the fungal cell wall, ultimately leading to osmotic instability and cell death.","source":"Toxin and Toxin Target Database (T3DB)","url":"http://www.t3db.ca/toxins/T3D2763"}]}],"manufacturing":[],"structure":true,"populations":{"noswallow":{"label":"No tablet or capsule to swallow","count":2,"forms":[["Powder for Reconstitution for I.V. infusion",1],["Powder for Concentrate for solution for i.v.Infusion",1]]}},"reg":{"fda":1,"ema":1,"phase":4.0,"first_approval":2006,"bbw":false,"control":null,"withdrawn":[],"reg_checked":true,"aware":null},"chem":{"props":{"key":"cid:166548","title":"Anidulafungin","products":2,"mw":1140.3,"logp":-0.93,"tpsa":377.4,"hbd":14,"hba":17,"rotb":14,"rings":7,"heavy":82,"lipinski":3,"formula":"C58H73N7O17"},"similar":[{"key":"cid:477468","title":"Micafungin","products":3,"mw":1270.3,"logp":-3.9,"tpsa":510.1,"hbd":16,"hba":23,"rotb":18,"rings":7,"heavy":89,"lipinski":